Fixed and committed as b8da66f3: a repo's .yoyo.toml can no longer pick the host your API key is sent to unless you trust the project. The full test suite, clippy with -D warnings, the fmt check and tests/harness_logic.sh (336/336) all pass.

What changed
• Census: of the 30 keys yoyo reads from the config file, only base_url names a host that receives a key. I didn't gate provider: with no URL set, the key only goes to that provider's own host.
• Two places read it: the main path (parse_model_config in src/cli.rs) and yoyo review (src/dispatch_sub.rs). Both now go through one gate in a new src/project_base_url.rs. review runs before the main trust decision, so it checks trust itself: the --trust-project / --trust-project-always flags or the remembered trust store.
• Untrusted project, provider with its own endpoint (anthropic, openai, ollama, …): yoyo ignores the project's URL, prints a warning naming the file and URL, and talks to that provider's own endpoint, which is where your key for it belongs.
• Untrusted project, `custom` or an unknown provider: yoyo refuses with exit 2 and "Nothing was sent". These providers have no endpoint of their own, so ignoring the URL would send the key to localhost:8080, a host nobody chose.
• The warning still prints under --quiet, because it says where your key goes.
• Trust prompt: the interactive trust question now lists base_url, so a "yes" there also lets the project use it.

One correction to the plan: it suggested telling users to "put base_url in your user config". That doesn't work. When a project has its own .yoyo.toml, yoyo reads only that file and skips the user-level config. So the message points to --trust-project or --base-url <url> instead.

Tests: tests/project_base_url_trust.rs uses a local recording stub and a fake key (canary-not-a-key), and never contacts a real vendor.
• Before the fix, I ran the three untrusted cases (custom refused, ollama ignored, review) on the old binary. All three failed, with the fake key showing up in the stub's records.
• Four cases must still reach the stub: --trust-project, the trust store (both -p and review), base_url in the user config, and the --base-url flag. They passed before the fix and still do.

Existing tests I changed: three tests (named_model_required.rs ×2, unknown_provider_warning_once.rs ×1) used a project .yoyo.toml with base_url. They now pass --trust-project so they still test that config path; their assertions are unchanged.

Checked by running vs. by reading only: I ran -p and yoyo review. I only read the code for the REPL, piped mode, side and sub-agents, /provider and --fallback. They all take the URL from the main path after it has been checked, and I didn't find any other path that still leaks the key, so I filed no new issue.

Docs: new sections in docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md and docs/src/configuration/permissions.md.

